  'd20b322d246': DhikrEnglish(
    subtitle: 'The complete ruqyah from the Quran and Sunnah',
    text:
        'The legitimate ruqyah is a lawful means of treatment and seeking cure, while the healing comes from Allah, Glory be to Him, the Most High. No one among the creation owns harm or benefit, so one must turn to Allah, Glory be to Him, the Most High, and not to anyone else.\n\n'
        'General guidelines to be observed in the legitimate ruqyah\n\n'
        'The one reciting should not believe that the ruqyah has an effect by itself, but only by Allah, Glory be to Him, the Most High.\n\n'
        'The reciter and the one receiving the ruqyah should be in a state of full purity.\n\n'
        'The reciter should face the qiblah.\n\n'
        'The reciter and the one receiving the ruqyah must reflect on the texts of the ruqyah. The reciter should not say them without thinking about their meanings, and the one receiving should listen only after striving to reflect on them. Both should bring humility during the ruqyah, with the heart attached to the great power of Allah the Most High and to seeking His help well.\n\n'
        'The reciter may limit the ruqyah to Quranic verses or Prophetic supplications for refuge, but the most complete is to combine them.\n\n'
        'The reciter may choose what suits the time available to them and to the one receiving the ruqyah. They may also shorten the ruqyah, choosing from it what suits the condition of the person, and may recite it in stages, so that the patient can rest between them.\n\n'
        'Gentle blowing, which is a light breath with a little saliva, during the recitation and after it, is allowed, and there is no harm in leaving it.\n\n'
        'It is recommended to place the hand, during the recitation, on the forehead or on the place of pain, noting that it is not permissible to touch women who are not one\'s mahram.\n\n'
        'If the reciter notices that the patient is affected by certain verses during the ruqyah, there is no harm in repeating them three, five or seven times, as needed and according to the level of response.\n\n'
        'The reciter should intend by the ruqyah to benefit their brother, love that Allah heals him and eases his burden, and also seek his guidance. If the reciter is certain that a jinn is possessing the person, they should work to free the person from that possession, while also being keen to invite that jinn to piety and uprightness. This is a very important aim that the reciter should keep in mind, since the greatest concern of a Muslim is calling to Allah the Most High, because of the words of the Lord, Mighty and Majestic: "Say: This is my way; I invite to Allah with insight, I and those who follow me." [Yusuf 108]. A Muslim is a caller to Allah first of all, so it is fitting that they perform the ruqyah carrying these two intentions in their heart (healing, and love of guidance). The reciter should also be aware that they should not seek to harm the jinn from the start, unless the ways of guiding him have been exhausted. How many a possessing jinn has repented and turned back at the hands of a reciter, and how many a rebellious devil has accepted Islam at their hands, so Allah the Most High wrote healing for the patient and guidance for the jinn.\n\n'
        'Take care to use the wording of the ruqyah that suits the situation when reciting, saying "I perform ruqyah for myself", "I perform ruqyah for you" (to a man or to a woman), or "I perform ruqyah for you all", according to the case.\n\n'
        'The ruqyah may continue for a whole week, and may be less than that or more, according to the condition of the patient and how they respond to the treatment, until healing is complete, by Allah\'s permission.\n\n'
        'If the reciter is sure that the person is suffering from magic (may Allah protect us), it is extremely important to focus the ruqyah on the verses in which magic is mentioned, repeating their recitation over the bewitched person, especially the two Mu\'awwidhat, for they have a great effect on undoing magic and warding off harm, by Allah\'s permission.\n\n'
        'The reciter may recite aloud or silently, and aloud is better, in a moderate voice that lets the patient hear it, so that they are more affected by the ruqyah and benefit from it more.',
  ),
